# Nothing in here is part of the app; all of it is the local machine talking to itself.
#
# `.env` is the reason this file exists. A source upload carries the working directory, and a Next.js
# process reads `.env` off its own filesystem at runtime - so a deployment built from an upload that
# included it would settle against a real processor with credentials nobody configured it with. The
# hosted floor is meant to run on the local one, and to say so; credentials belong in the platform's
# own environment, where they can be named, rotated and removed.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Local artefacts, none of which the build reads.
shots/
tools/phrase-blocklist.json
test-results/
playwright-report/
coverage/
*.log
